{{!-- custom-institution-guide: /institutions/guide/.
     Two audiences on one page, labelled, because the administrator and
     the person just trying to read an essay need completely different
     things and a single undifferentiated list serves neither.
     The librarian section exists because Patrick Henry's library
     director asked about IP access directly and she will not be the
     last. --}}

<main class="membership membership-support">

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Organizational Membership</p>
      <h1 class="page-hero-headline"><em>Getting your people</em> reading.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero-sub">A short guide for administrators and for everyone they cover.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="form-section">
    <div class="container">

      {{!-- Video slot. CSP already permits youtube-nocookie and Vimeo in
            frame-src, so no header change is needed to fill this in.
            Left as a comment rather than an empty iframe so the page
            doesn't render a grey box before the video exists. --}}
      {{!-- <div class="guide-video">
        <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/VIDEO_ID"
                title="How organizational membership works"
                loading="lazy" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen></iframe>
      </div> --}}

      <div class="institutional-overview">
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>How access works</h3>
          <p>By email domain. Anyone with an address at your organization's domain can create a full membership. There are no seats to assign and nothing to install.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>What everyone gets</h3>
          <p>The full archive, the ebook library, audio versions of every essay, the print journal's digital editions, and the members' forum.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>How long it lasts</h3>
          <p>One year from purchase. We will email you before it ends. Nothing renews automatically.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-heading"><em>If you administer the membership</em></h2>

      <div class="form-group">
        <p class="form-intro"><strong>1. Send your people the link and the code.</strong> Both arrived in your confirmation email. The link looks like <code>mereorthodoxy.com/join/?i=your-organization</code> and the code is eight characters. Anyone with an email address at your domain can use them.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>2. Put them somewhere people will actually look.</strong> A staff email, the intranet, a course page, the weekly bulletin. This is the step that decides whether the membership gets used, and it is the one most often skipped.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>3. Add more domains if you need them.</strong> Many organizations have more than one, and a second campus or a legacy domain will not work until it is added. Manage this at <a href="/institution-manage/">your organization page</a>, using the link from your confirmation email. That link lasts fourteen days; email us for a fresh one.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>4. Check who has signed up.</strong> The same page lists everyone who has joined, so you can see whether it is being used before renewal comes around.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>5. Renewal is a conversation.</strong> We will reach out before your year is up. Nothing is charged automatically, and you will never be surprised by a card payment.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-heading"><em>If someone sent you a link</em></h2>

      <div class="form-group">
        <p class="form-intro"><strong>Use your organization's email address.</strong> That is what identifies you as covered. A personal Gmail address will not be recognised, even if you use it for everything else.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>Enter the access code your administrator gave you.</strong> Then you are done. There is nothing to pay and no trial to cancel.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>Signing in later:</strong> use <em>Sign in</em> at the top of any page and enter the same address. We email you a link rather than asking for a password.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-heading"><em>For librarians</em></h2>

      <div class="form-group">
        <p class="form-intro"><strong>We do not use IP-based or proxy authentication.</strong> This is the question we are asked most, so it is worth stating plainly: there is no IP range to register, no EZproxy stanza to write, and no SAML configuration. Access is by email domain, which means a patron off campus has exactly the same access as one in the building, with no VPN.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>Linking from a course page or LibGuide:</strong> link directly to the article URL. A covered reader who is signed in reaches it normally. One who is not sees a prompt to sign in with their institutional address.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>Course reserves and coursepacks:</strong> email us. We are a small publication and generally glad to grant permission for classroom use; we would simply like to know where our writers' work is going.</p>

        <p class="form-intro"><strong>The print journal is separate from the membership.</strong> We can add copies for the library to your agreement. Email us with how many you would like.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="section-heading"><em>Anything else</em></h2>

      <div class="form-group">
        <p class="form-intro">Email <a href="mailto:ian@mereorthodoxy.com">ian@mereorthodoxy.com</a>. A person answers.</p>
        <p class="form-intro"><a href="/institutions/">Back to organizational membership</a></p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</main>
